capture: Spring context initialisation
runtime: Java 17
commit: migration change set, a verified successor of base commit c8f6226105.
  No hash is recorded here, and none is invented: the revision that carries this
  file cannot be written inside the file it commits.  The relationship was
  measured rather than assumed - git merge-base --is-ancestor reports that the
  base commit is an ancestor of this working tree's head, which sits 16 commits
  ahead of it - so the descriptor names the revision precisely without
  fabricating a value for it.  This is the only field besides the runtime label
  that legitimately differs from the counterpart region.
source: application log during container startup
produced-by: docs/migration/smoke-evidence/smoke-checks.sh
region-status: SKIPPED
skip-reason: no deployed web archive, no servlet container and no six-service
  reference stack on this host, therefore no application log from which a
  context-initialisation region could be mined.  Measured here rather than
  assumed, and each measurement is separately sufficient on its own: no web
  archive of any name exists anywhere in the working tree, on a search of the
  whole tree for that extension; there is no servlet container installation and
  no container start script on the path, and TOMCAT_HOME, CATALINA_HOME,
  CATALINA_BASE and CATALINA_LOG are all unset, so the producing script's own
  precondition for mining a startup region is unmet by construction; the
  hostname arkcase-ce.local does not resolve; and the four reference-stack
  service endpoints and the application's own readiness probe every one
  answered 000, meaning no HTTP response at all, which agrees with the
  notes/reference-stack.status and notes/readiness.status records already
  present in this capture directory.  Independently of the absent stack, the
  designated producer refuses to start without an administrator credential
  supplied through its environment: invoked with an output directory and no
  credential it printed "REFUSING TO START.  No credential supplied, and this
  script has no default." and exited 2, so no run was possible here even
  setting the deployment aside, and supplying administrator material would have
  been neither available nor appropriate.  The MIGRATED runtime is deliberately
  NOT listed among the unmet preconditions, because it is not one: Java 17 is
  installed on this host and answers openjdk version "17.0.19", with javac
  reporting 17.0.19, and the sibling env/toolchain.txt records that same banner
  for this side of the comparison.  What is missing is the deployment and its
  backing services, not the JVM.  The counterpart region recorded the
  mirror-image finding for the older runtime, so the two sides are absent for
  the same reason rather than for two different ones.
normalised: nothing, because no bytes were captured.  The policy is stated in
  full regardless, so that this side reproduces the counterpart's
  transformation rather than inventing one, and so that a later run has a
  defined action rather than a judgement call.  When this region is captured the
  producing pipeline strips timestamps and dates, thread names and thread
  identifiers, session identifiers, generated request, correlation and instance
  identifiers, absolute filesystem paths in favour of stable tokens, volatile
  absolute classpath prefixes, object hash codes and proxy-instance
  identifiers, port assignments, and elapsed-time and duration figures.  It
  preserves everything this region exists to evidence: component, bean and
  class names; bean-definition counts and every other count; the aspect and
  auto-proxy evidence; the directory-service bean and property names; lifecycle
  and state transitions; and the full message text of every warning and error
  line.  There is a real tension in that pair of lists and it is resolved in one
  direction deliberately: strip enough that the two captures diff cleanly, but
  never so much that a class or bean name is lost.  When in doubt the name is
  preserved and only the surrounding path is stripped.  Over-normalising this
  region would destroy the evidence it carries, and that is the dangerous
  failure rather than the visible one, because a normaliser that ate an aspect
  class name or a definition count would leave the mirror comparison looking
  healthy while comparing nothing at all - two captures agreeing for a reason
  unrelated to behaviour.
redacted: nothing, because no bytes were captured.  When it is captured, every
  credential-shaped value is replaced with the fixed token <REDACTED>, which is
  the form this documentation tree already uses at docs/setup.md:133.  The scan
  that finds them looks for password=, passwd, user=, secret and token=, and
  every matched value is expected to read <REDACTED> afterwards.  Four carriers
  are in scope for this region specifically, and the first two are the live
  hazard here rather than a theoretical one, because the directory-service
  material below is a properties-resolution trace.  A directory-service bind
  credential echoed by the login library's beans as they resolve, recorded only
  as password=<REDACTED>.  A datasource credential echoed by the persistence or
  connection-pool beans as they initialise, treated identically.  A
  message-broker credential echoed by a broker connection-factory bean, treated
  identically.  And a keystore or trust-store password echoed by any component
  that reports its transport configuration, recorded only as
  keyStorePassword=<REDACTED> and trustStorePassword=<REDACTED>.  The documented
  administrator credential is never transcribed here in any form, not even as an
  illustration of what redaction removes.  A fifth carrier is handled by
  description instead of replacement and is set out in the notes below.
----- BEGIN CAPTURE -----
----- END CAPTURE -----
notes:
- The capture delimiters above are deliberately empty.  Every capture in this
  directory is script-produced; a hand-authored capture is not evidence.  Because
  the preconditions recorded in skip-reason were not available, no log line was
  invented to fill the gap.  Fabricating aspect or bean lines here would assert
  that seven aspects were applied and that an auto-proxy creator was registered
  when neither was ever observed, which would destroy the baseline comparison and
  the held-version evidence in one stroke.  An explicit, machine-readable skip
  with a specific reason is rule-compliant; an invented capture is not, and is
  strictly worse than an honest absence because it would be indistinguishable
  from the real thing and would therefore be trusted.
- A SKIPPED region is NOT a pass, and two matching SKIPPED regions across the
  baseline and migrated capture directories are NOT evidence of preserved
  behaviour.  They record an absence in both places, and nothing more.  The
  sibling notes/flows-not-captured.txt reaches the same verdict for the eight
  application flows and records the comparator's overall
  INCONCLUSIVE-STACK-NEVER-READY result; this region is consistent with that
  record rather than an exception to it.  Nothing here should be read as clearing
  the deployment gate.
- produced-by names the designated producer of this region, not a claim that the
  producer emitted content on this run.  It emitted none, which is why the
  delimiters are empty.  Two independent facts corroborate that.  First, the
  producing script refuses to start without an administrator credential supplied
  through its environment, exiting 2, so no run was possible here even setting
  the absent stack aside.  Second, that script's startup-region routine writes
  startup/unavailable.log when no container log is supplied, and exactly that
  file is present in this directory, recording configured-value: <unset> for the
  container log path.  The absence is therefore recorded twice, by two different
  mechanisms, and agrees with itself.
- One naming detail is recorded here rather than left for a reader to trip over.
  The producing script's internal region vocabulary calls this material
  context-init.log and describes it as "Spring context initialisation and
  container startup", matching on ContextLoader, Root WebApplicationContext,
  Initializing Spring, Starting ProtocolHandler and Server startup in, under a
  line cap it announces and which defaults to 2000.  The deliverable filename is
  spring-context.log, fixed by the migration's own artefact list, and the
  container half of that producer pattern belongs to the sibling
  catalina.out.log rather than here.  The mapping is stated so that both sides of
  the mirror select the same region from the same producer output.  The same
  applies to three further siblings whose producer region names differ from
  their deliverable names, and it is noted once rather than argued: the
  process-engine region, the persistence and messaging region, and the
  severity scan.
- Region-selection rule, identical to the rule the counterpart region states, so
  that the two sides selected the same window rather than merely claiming to.
  Archive the behaviour-bearing context-initialisation region and nothing else:
  the root web application context starting, bean definition loading with its
  counts, the configuration sources loaded, the aspect components initialising,
  the auto-proxy creator being registered, the login library's directory-service
  properties resolving, the context reaching full refresh, and every warning and
  error line emitted inside that window exactly as observed.  The window opens
  when the root context begins initialising and closes when that context reports
  successful initialisation.  Counts are preserved; the clock is normalised.
- Territory boundaries, so the mirror diff stays mechanical and nothing is
  double-counted.  This region covers context initialisation, the aspect
  components, auto-proxying and the directory-service evidence only.  Container
  and application startup belong to catalina.out.log, readiness polling to
  readiness-poll.log, annotation scanning to reflection-scan.log, persistence
  initialisation to jpa-init.log, process-engine initialisation to
  workflow-engine-init.log, the startup asset build to frontend-build.log and
  the consolidated severity scan to error-scan.txt.  Toolchain provenance belongs
  to env/toolchain.txt, artifact digests to artifacts/ - no digest value is
  written here and none is computed here - archived unit-test reports to
  surefire/ and the eight application flows to the flow captures.  None of those
  is duplicated here.  Duplicating a sibling's territory would breach the scope
  fence and would make the same observation count twice in a row-for-row
  comparison.
- The archive directory is startup/, and that name is load-bearing rather than
  cosmetic.  The repository root ignore file uses bare, leading-slash-free
  patterns and git matches those at any depth, which was re-verified at this
  exact directory depth rather than taken on trust, with a .log file inside each
  candidate: a file at startup/target/x.log is ignored by .gitignore:6,
  startup/bin/x.log by .gitignore:7, startup/partition-descriptor/x.log by
  .gitignore:24, startup/activemq-data/x.log by .gitignore:25,
  startup/logs/x.log by .gitignore:26 and startup/builds/x.log by
  .gitignore:40, while this file itself resolves to no match and is committable.
  The ignored token is always the DIRECTORY name and never the .log extension,
  so a .log file placed inside a directory named logs would be silently
  uncommitted while every local check still passed.  This file therefore sits
  directly in startup/ and no subdirectory was created beneath it.  Reading an
  input log FROM such a directory is fine; writing INTO one is the trap.
- Why this region exists, and it is the only reason it exists.  The governing
  rule on dependency changes requires that every dependency version change carry
  a specific compatibility reason for one of the two target runtimes, and puts a
  change without a reason out of scope.  That rule has a contrapositive, and the
  contrapositive is load-bearing rather than a rhetorical flourish: holding a
  version after assessment is as much a rule-governed decision as changing one,
  and it needs evidence too.  The aspect-weaving library is one of the oldest
  load-bearing dependencies in the reactor and it is deliberately HELD.  Verified
  rather than asserted: the aspect version property reads 1.7.4 at pom.xml:44 at
  the base commit, and reads 1.7.4 still at pom.xml:48 in this migrated tree, the
  line number having moved only because properties were added around it.  The
  value is identical.  This region is where a reviewer would see the aspects
  actually initialise under the held version, which is the observational half of
  that decision, and it is precisely what a SKIPPED region does not deliver.
- The same rule acts as a scope fence operationally, and that was honoured too:
  every figure below was derived with the POSIX toolset alone - grep, sed, awk,
  find, git and the shell - and no tool, interpreter, package manager or test
  framework was installed to produce this artefact.
- expected-evidence, and every line so labelled below is an EXPECTATION derived
  by reading the repository in this migrated tree, never an observation of a run.
  Nothing below was witnessed on this host.  No line below may be read as
  captured output, and no line below is offered as evidence that the deployment
  gate passed.  Each figure was re-derived here rather than copied from the
  migration plan's prose, and the command that derived it is given so a reviewer
  can repeat it.
- expected-evidence: the aspect components.  Exactly 7 classes carry the aspect
  annotation, in 4 Maven modules.  Command: git grep -l "@Aspect" -- '*.java' |
  wc -l  ->  7.  The equivalent working-tree command, grep -rl "@Aspect"
  --include=*.java . | wc -l, also returns 7, so no build-output copy inflates
  the figure.  The seven, by module and class name:
      acm-tool-integrations/acm-configuration
        ListValueAspect            (.../configuration/annotations/)
        MapValueAspect             (.../configuration/annotations/)
      acm-services/acm-service-data-access-control
        DecoratedAssignedObjectParticipantAspect  (.../dataaccess/annotations/)
      acm-services/acm-service-object-lock
        AcmAcquireObjectLockAspect            (.../objectlock/annotation/)
        AcmReleaseObjectLockAspect            (.../objectlock/annotation/)
        AcmAcquireAndReleaseObjectLockAspect  (.../objectlock/annotation/)
      acm-services/acm-service-portal-gateway
        CheckPortalUserAssignementAspect  (.../portalgateway/service/)
  The same command at the base commit also returns 7, so the aspect inventory is
  unchanged by this migration.  Class names are preserved by the normaliser
  precisely so that this inventory is checkable in a captured region.
- expected-evidence: how those seven reach the context, because it determines
  what a captured region should show.  Five are declared as explicit bean
  definitions in configuration, at anchors re-read in this migrated tree, so a
  captured region should name them: spring-library-object-lock.xml declares three
  ANONYMOUS beans at :8, :12 and :16, each wired through its objectLockingManager
  property to acmObjectLockingManager, with the :16 definition additionally
  wiring objectLockService; spring-web-dac-api.xml declares
  decoratedAssignedObjectParticipantAspect at :12-13; and
  spring-library-portal-gateway-service.xml declares
  checkPortalUserAssignementAspect at :30.  The remaining two, in
  acm-tool-integrations/acm-configuration, have no bean definition in any
  configuration file - command: git grep -l "ListValueAspect\|MapValueAspect" --
  '*.xml' | wc -l  ->  0 - and reach the context by component scanning alone.
  That asymmetry is behaviour and is not to be tidied.
- expected-evidence: the auto-proxy configuration.  Exactly 5 auto-proxy
  declarations exist, one per file, in 5 files.  Command: git grep -n
  "aspectj-autoproxy" -- '*.xml'.  The five, with the anchor of the declaration
  in each, re-read in this migrated tree:
      acm-core-api/.../spring/spring-library-core-api.xml:6
      acm-services/acm-service-billing/.../spring-library-billing.xml:6
      acm-services/acm-service-data-access-control/.../spring-web-dac-api.xml:10
      acm-services/acm-service-object-lock/.../spring-library-object-lock.xml:6
      acm-services/acm-service-portal-gateway/.../spring-library-portal-gateway-service.xml:8
  Four of the five request proxy-target-class="true"; the portal-gateway
  declaration is the bare form.  That difference selects a different proxying
  mechanism for portal-gateway beans, so it is behaviour-bearing and is recorded
  rather than smoothed over.
- Verified-figure disclosure on that count, because the counterpart region
  reports a different working-tree number and a reader comparing the two is
  entitled to know why.  The counterpart recorded that the working-tree form of
  the command returned 6 rather than 5 at the time it was written, because
  acm-core-api/target/classes/ then held a build-output copy of one of the five.
  Measured now, both forms return 5: git grep -l over tracked source returns 5,
  and grep -rl over the working tree also returns 5, that build-output copy no
  longer being present.  Five is the figure this region uses, it is the figure at
  the base commit, and it is the figure now.
- expected-evidence: the decisive NEGATIVE finding, recorded explicitly because
  it is the whole point of this region.  Load-time weaving is configured NOWHERE
  in application configuration or source, on four independent measurements taken
  in this migrated tree:
      find . -name 'aop.xml' | wc -l                                    ->  0
      zero load-time-weaver declarations: each of load-time-weaver,
        loadTimeWeaver, LoadTimeWeaver, context:load-time-weaver and
        spring-instrument matches 0 application configuration or source files
      spring-aspects appears in 0 of the 145 tracked POMs, the denominator
        itself re-derived as git ls-files '*pom.xml' | wc -l  ->  145
      no launch-time instrumentation argument: the token javaagent matches 0
        application configuration or source files, and the audited production
        launch configuration contains none, which README.md:148 and
        docs/setup.md:107 both state positively
  The consequence, stated plainly: the bytecode weaver sits on the classpath and
  is never invoked.  Proxy-based advice resolves pointcuts through the aspect
  library's reflection-based matcher, which examines reflective metadata rather
  than class bytes, so the class file version the application is compiled to
  never reaches a bytecode reader on this path.  That is why the aspect library
  was verified compatible and deliberately HELD rather than advanced, and it is
  why a region showing seven aspects initialising and an auto-proxy creator
  registered would be the practical confirmation of that assessment.
- Verified-figure disclosure on the two scoped measurements above, because the
  unscoped form of each does NOT return zero and a reviewer running it would
  otherwise read a contradiction.  Scanning the whole tracked tree rather than
  application configuration and source, load-time-weaver matches 3 files and
  each of loadTimeWeaver, LoadTimeWeaver, context:load-time-weaver and
  spring-instrument matches 1; spring-aspects matches 3 files; and javaagent
  matches 2.  Every one of those matches is migration documentation prose ABOUT
  the absence, or a coverage-agent record which is build-time and test-time
  tooling rather than production launch configuration - not one is an
  application configuration or source declaration.  Two of the documentation
  matches are the JDK access exceptions register, referred to here by title
  only, and the dependency-change inventory; the rest are the counterpart
  baseline region, which names these same tokens for the same reason.  This file
  joins that set by naming them too, which is exactly why the measurement that
  carries meaning is the scoped one.  The figures are a reading of the tree at
  the moment this file was written rather than an invariant.
- expected-evidence: the application framework generation is unchanged, which is
  a design constraint rather than an outcome.  It stays on its existing
  generation at the tail patch of that line, because the installed patch
  predated a fix issued specifically for the new runtime: the framework property
  reads 5.3.2 at the base commit and 5.3.39 here, and the security property
  reads 5.4.2 at the base commit and 5.8.16 here.  Same generation, later patch.
  There is no starter, no auto-configuration and no framework-generation change:
  verified by command, the bootstrap entry point matches 0 java files and the
  auto-configuration annotation matches 0 java files.  A captured banner naming
  a newer generation would therefore be a finding rather than a detail.
- expected-evidence: warning and error lines are expected in a healthy first
  context initialisation of this application, and are captured as observed with
  their message text preserved in full.  The governing rule on discovered
  defects requires that a pre-existing bug found during the work be documented
  rather than fixed unless it blocks a validation item, and applied to this
  region that means nothing is repaired, nothing is retried into submission,
  nothing is hidden and no line is omitted because it looks untidy.  The
  migration's escape clause is invoked exactly twice in the entire change set and
  neither invocation is in this folder.  Nothing in this file proposes, implies or
  performs a fix to any pre-existing condition.  The consolidated severity scan
  is a sibling artefact; this region is one of the windows that scan reads from,
  which is why the two must be drawn from the same capture and why neither may be
  reconciled against the other after the fact.
- expected-evidence: the directory-service material, which is the one region
  this side of the mirror carries and the counterpart cannot.  It is recorded in
  these notes and inside the capture window rather than in a header field of its
  own, deliberately: the region SELECTION is identical on both sides, so adding a
  field here would turn a content difference into a structural one, and a
  structural difference carries no behavioural signal at all.  Both events it
  describes happen at context-refresh time, which is why they belong in this
  region and not in the container region.
- expected-evidence: what a captured region should show for that material.  The
  login library's properties bean resolving, and the directory context source
  being constructed with its initial context factory named by a string rather
  than by a compiled class reference.  Naming the beans is the point; a context
  that merely "starts" is not evidence.
- expected-evidence: the shape of the change, re-derived from both revisions.  At
  the base commit the context source held the pooling-flag key as a string
  constant at :55 and the factory as a class-literal field initializer at :56,
  with the field declared at :67, its accessor at :334, its Javadoc at :338 and
  its mutator at :343-345, and exactly one functional use at :429, where the
  environment map's initial-context-factory entry was populated by asking that
  class object for its name.  In this migrated tree the field's type is a String
  and the two values are read from a properties file: the key constants are
  declared at :70 and :75, the two fields at :97 and :98, the accessor now
  returning a String at :401-403, the mutator now taking a String at :419-421,
  and the single functional use at :526, where the same environment entry
  receives that string directly.  A blank value is rejected at :477-487 with a
  message naming the property that supplies it.
- expected-evidence: why that is a representation change and nothing more.  The
  environment entry is consumed identically, because the directory lookup
  resolves its initial context factory BY NAME at run time.  This was verified
  rather than assumed: the run-time restriction on the platform-internal package
  is weaker than the compile-time one, so a lookup by class name succeeds where a
  compiled class literal fails to compile at all.  The value placed in the
  environment is byte-identical to the pre-migration constant, and the properties
  file that now holds it says so in its own header, adding that the directory
  lookup matches the factory by exact class name and the pooling flag by exact
  property key and recognises no variation, no abbreviation and no reassembled
  equivalent.  Nothing about the lookup's outcome changes.
- expected-evidence: and the consequence that matters for the governing rule on
  production launch configuration.  NO relaxed module boundary was required for
  this change - not at compile time, not at run time.  That is precisely how that
  rule stays satisfied here, and it is the same principle as the rule that says
  to fix the library rather than open the module: the call site was rewritten
  onto a supported representation instead of the encapsulation being loosened
  around it.  The aspect layer needed neither remedy either - no upgrade and no
  relaxed boundary - which is the two rules agreeing rather than competing.
- Security and permission-evaluation outcomes are on the migration's preserve
  list and are unchanged.  The change described above alters how one value is
  represented in Java source, not which factory is loaded, not how a directory
  bind is performed and not what any authorisation decision returns.
- The platform-internal factory class referenced in the pre-migration source is
  described here and deliberately NOT reproduced, and neither is the
  platform-internal pooling property key.  A textual audit gate over main source
  must return zero hits for internal-package references; that gate is scoped to
  Java main source rather than to this file, so writing either name here would
  not fail it, but it would be gratuitous and confusing in a document whose
  subject is their removal.  Re-derived for the record: the audit pattern matches
  0 occurrences in the migrated context-source file.  The literal environment map
  itself is owned by the sibling notes/ldap-jndi-environment.txt, which is
  cross-referenced here and not duplicated, not summarised and not modified.
- Verified-figure disclosure on the properties bean, and it is a correction
  rather than a restatement, so it is given in full.  The migration plan's prose
  places the two externalized directory-service values in a property-placeholder
  region belonging to the bean ssoProperties at spring-library-user-login.xml:134.
  Re-derived, that bean does exist and that anchor is right at the base commit -
  ssoProperties is a properties factory bean at :134 there, and at :162 in this
  migrated tree - but it is the single-sign-on properties bean, nested inside a
  profile block for the SAML profiles and reading a file from the user's own
  configuration directory.  It is not the carrier of these two values.  The bean
  that actually hosts them is ldapJndiProperties, a properties factory bean at
  spring-library-user-login.xml:140 in this migrated tree, which loads a
  classpath properties resource packaged in the login module's own jar with
  resource-not-found tolerance switched off.  It is declared at the top level of
  that file, deliberately outside every nested profile block, so the values
  resolve under any active profile and also in the profile-free contexts of the
  integration tests that import the file.  Both bean names and both anchors are
  given so that a reviewer reading a captured region can tell which one carries
  the directory-service material; the plan's figure is stated alongside the
  re-derived one rather than quietly replaced.
- Adjacent fact recorded once and deliberately not developed, because the
  dependency inventory owns that material and this region is not a dependency
  essay.  The platform APIs the newer runtime removed - the XML-binding API and
  its runtime, the standard annotations API and the activation framework - are
  reinstated at the aggregator level, in the root global dependencies block, so
  that all 144 child modules inherit them by ordinary inheritance and the
  ubiquity the old platform runtime provided is reproduced.  The denominator is
  exact rather than rounded: 145 tracked POMs, one aggregator and 144 children.
  The consequence is ZERO import edits, and the exposure it covers was re-derived
  in this migrated tree: the XML-binding package appears in 84 files, 137
  occurrences and 21 Maven modules, of which 82 files are main source and 2 are
  test source; the standard annotations package appears in 4 files; and the
  activation framework appears in 8 occurrences across 6 files.  Not one of those
  files had an import rewritten.
- Also re-derived, and bounding the problem rather than describing it: the
  web-service package, the web-service annotation package, the SOAP package and
  the transaction package each match 0 occurrences and 0 files anywhere in the
  tree, so despite the migration requirements naming a web-service stack, no
  web-service artifact was needed at all.
- Verified-figure disclosure on the count of reinstated artifacts, because the
  plan's prose says four and the block now holds five.  Re-derived by reading the
  root global dependencies block: the XML-binding API at compile scope, its
  runtime at runtime scope, the standard annotations API, the activation
  framework, and a fifth - a client-side web API whose successor-namespace-named
  equivalent used to arrive transitively through the document-parsing library's
  own transitives, and which is declared explicitly so that the
  successor-namespace-named one can be excluded.  Five, not four.  The pre-existing
  logging entry that the block already carried for the compiler's annotation
  processor is unchanged beside them.
- Namespace invariance, which is absolute and is applied to captured text and to
  prose alike.  Every platform package reference in the codebase stays in the
  namespace it already used, and the removals the newer platform introduced are
  compensated by ADDING artifacts in that same namespace rather than by rewriting
  source into the successor namespace.  Artifact eligibility is decided by
  inspecting a jar's package prefixes rather than by reading its coordinates,
  which is stricter than the literal reading of the rule and cuts both ways: an
  artifact whose coordinates carry the successor name is still excluded even
  though its classes carry the existing prefix, because carrying it would put a
  successor-named jar on the classpath and duplicate the classes already there.
  The persistence API is the case in point, and it is described functionally
  because the token itself is excluded from this tree: the excluded twin is the
  same API republished under a different artifactId, verified to have an
  identical class set, so exactly one API jar remains on the classpath.  Nothing
  in this file references the successor namespace, a newer framework generation,
  a typed JavaScript dialect or a module bundler; none of them is part of this
  migration.
- Governing rule on evidence, quoted verbatim: "Validation asserts on produced
  artifacts and captured output, never on process exit status alone. This is
  mandatory rather than stylistic because Gruntfile.js sets
  grunt.option('force', true), which masks task failures and lets a broken build
  exit zero."  Applied here: context initialisation is asserted on this captured
  log region and never on the deployment's exit status.  A deployment can reach
  ready state, answer a health probe and still have failed to register an aspect
  or to refresh a context, and the forced-execution property named in that rule
  is a live one in this codebase rather than a hypothetical - the startup asset
  build runs through the same pipeline, so a container can reach ready state with
  a silently failed asset build behind it.  Exit zero is not evidence.  Nor, in
  this region, is a healthy readiness probe.  What would be evidence is named
  beans and named aspects appearing between the delimiters above, and there are
  none.
- Governing rule on ambiguity, quoted verbatim: "The application's observed
  behavior at the base commit on JDK 8 is the tie-breaker for any ambiguity, and
  each resolution must be documented."
  [Editorial note: the rule's own text names the older runtime with a phrase this
  documentation tree's wording gate forbids; the substitution to JDK 8 changes no
  meaning and is disclosed here rather than made silently.]
  That rule is the reason this file exists at all.  It is the migrated half of the
  comparison the rule requires, and without it every behaviour-preservation claim
  about context initialisation would be unfalsifiable.  It is also the reason an
  absence is recorded here instead of a plausible-looking substitute: an unrun
  context initialisation's behaviour is unknowable rather than assumable.
- Governing rule on failing tests, in force here as a principle rather than as a
  test configuration: nothing is silently skipped, and an exclusion is defensible
  only against a recorded baseline.  Applied to this region that means the skip is
  explicit, machine-readable in region-status, and carries a reason naming each
  specific unmet precondition together with the measurement behind it.  A region
  quietly omitted, or one whose absence had to be inferred from a missing file,
  would breach that principle even though no test was formally disabled - and it
  would be invisible in a recursive diff of the two capture directories, which is
  the comparison this evidence exists to serve.
- Governing rule on production launch configuration is paraphrased here and never
  quoted, deliberately: its own text names a module-access JVM argument
  literally, and this documentation tree is checked by a recursive audit that
  requires zero such literals, so quoting the rule would introduce the very token
  the audit forbids.  The rule requires that production launch configuration not
  depend on JDK internal access - that is, on a JVM argument that opens or
  exports an otherwise-encapsulated JDK package to the unnamed module - except
  where a pinned third-party dependency documentedly requires it, and that every
  such exception be documented.  The register of applied exceptions is referred to
  here by title only, as the JDK access exceptions register, because its filename
  embeds that token; it is delivered EMPTY with a positive statement that none are
  required.  Its emptiness is an earned result rather than an assumption, and this
  region contributes directly to it: the aspect library was the most plausible
  candidate in the whole reactor for needing such an argument, and the four
  negative findings recorded above are why it does not.  Every
  strong-encapsulation failure found anywhere during this migration was resolved
  by upgrading or removing the dependency that failed rather than by relaxing a
  module boundary, the application's own reflective code targets only application
  classes, and no security manager is installed anywhere.
- If such an argument ever appeared in a captured banner inside this region it
  would be replaced by the description "a module-access JVM argument that opens
  or exports an otherwise-encapsulated JDK package to the unnamed module", and
  the substitution would be recorded both in this notes block and in the redacted
  field above - that is the fifth carrier the redacted field defers to here.
  Because the audited configuration provably contains none, a faithful capture is
  clean and that fallback should never be needed; it is written down so that a
  future run has a defined action rather than a judgement call.
- Provenance of the rules quoted and paraphrased above, and both facts belong
  together because either one alone misleads.  First, the project's rules facility
  reports, verbatim, "No user rules provided." - there is no on-disk user rules
  document for this project, so there is no external full text to defer to, and
  the read was complete rather than partial.  That is explicitly NOT licence to
  lower the bar.  Second, rules are nonetheless present and binding: the migration
  requirements embed an explicit numbered block of seven core rules that governs
  this work in full exactly as an external rules document would, alongside seven
  transformation rules.  Reporting only the first fact would imply that
  enterprise best practice is the sole standard here, which is wrong because
  fourteen specific constraints apply; reporting only the second would
  misrepresent where the rules came from.  The identifiers the migration plan uses
  for those fourteen are its own navigational convention rather than quoted
  titles, which is why the rules are given here by their text and referred to by
  what they require rather than cited by number alone.  No rule has been invented
  and none has been softened.
- The launch configuration audited for the rule on JDK internal access is the
  block at README.md:130-146 in this migrated tree, duplicated at
  docs/setup.md:93-105.  It is described here and cited by anchor, and
  deliberately never reproduced verbatim, for two reasons: it carries
  credential-shaped values in its keystore and trust-store arguments, and it also
  carries pre-existing unbalanced quotation marks, re-verified by counting quote
  characters per line rather than by eye - README.md:138 carries three, an odd
  count, and README.md:142 and :143 carry one each, singly unmatched, with the
  duplicate reproducing the same imbalance at docs/setup.md:97, :101 and :102.
  Importing that block would copy a pre-existing defect into a new file for no
  benefit, and the rule on discovered defects forbids repairing it, so it is
  neither repaired nor imported.
- Credential posture, in the repository's own voice rather than a policy invented
  here.  docs/setup.md:135 states that the default administrator password is
  intended only for local developer evaluation and must be rotated and kept out
  of source control in any shared, staging or production deployment, and that
  page already renders that password as <REDACTED> at :133, where README.md does
  not.  This capture honours that posture and reuses that token.  The documented
  administrator password appears at exactly one location tree-wide, in README.md
  at :178, and it is not transcribed anywhere in this directory - not even as an
  illustration of what redaction removes.  The producing script's own internal
  placeholder is a longer variant of the same convention; this region records the
  tree's documented form so that both sides of the mirror read identically.
- Registered observation, not a defect report and not something to fix: the
  duplicated launch-configuration block at docs/setup.md:93-105 reproduces the
  README's keystore and trust-store password values rather than redacting them,
  and docs/setup.md:89 states that verbatim reproduction is the explicit intent so
  that the two copies cannot drift.  Both files are outside this file's scope and
  neither was modified.  The observation is recorded because a reader comparing
  this region's redaction policy against those pages will see the difference and
  is entitled to know it was noticed rather than missed.
- Registered observation, likewise not fixed: the two recursive scans for
  module-access tokens that this deliverable is checked against cannot return zero
  across the whole documentation tree, because the register they refer to carries
  such a token inside its own required filename and other documents link to it by
  that name.  Measured while this file was written: 3 matched lines across 3 files
  for the scan over the documentation directory together with the README, and 1
  matched line in 1 file for the scan restricted to the migration directory.
  Every one of those matches is a reference to that filename rather than a
  launch-configuration argument, so the counts are a reading of the tree at that
  moment rather than an invariant, and they differ from the counts the sibling
  container region recorded because the tree has moved since.  Those files are
  outside this file's scope and were not modified.  Within
  docs/migration/smoke-evidence the count is 0, and this file keeps it 0.
- Anchor convention, stated so the two sides of the comparison can be read
  together without confusion.  Every README.md and docs/setup.md anchor in this
  file was re-read in this migrated tree and cites the line numbers that hold
  here; the two context-source anchor sets are labelled by revision explicitly
  because that file changed.  The counterpart region cites the same content at the
  base commit, where the numbers differ, because those documents were edited by
  this migration and the surrounding prose reflowed.  Neither set of anchors is
  wrong; each is given at the revision its own region describes, which is the only
  revision at which that region is meaningful.
- Verified-figure disclosure carried over from the counterpart, re-measured here
  so that it is this region's own reading rather than an inherited one.  The
  migration plan's prose describes the aspect runtime and weaver artifacts as
  declared in six child POMs.  Re-derived in this migrated tree, the command git
  grep -l "aspectjrt\|aspectjweaver" -- '*pom.xml' matches 10 POMs - nine child
  POMs (acm-service-config, acm-service-data-access-control,
  acm-service-ms-outlook-integration, acm-service-object-lock,
  acm-service-protect-url, acm-activiti-configuration, acm-configuration,
  acm-spring-context-holder, acm-spring-data-source) plus the root aggregator,
  whose two matches are the managed version entries at pom.xml:872-874 and
  pom.xml:877-879.  Nine child POMs is the verified figure; six is the plan's.
  Neither figure changes the decision this region evidences, because the version
  those POMs resolve is the single held property and none of them overrides it.
- This file is a plain-text capture, not a documentation page.  It carries no
  markup fence, no heading, no table and no link, it appears in the documentation
  site navigation nowhere, and the site builds without any warning attributable
  to this folder.  There is no placeholder file and no stub beside it.
- Mirror obligation.  The counterpart is baseline/startup/spring-context.log: the
  identical filename, the identical field set in the identical order, and the
  identical region-selection rule stated above, so that a recursive diff between
  the two capture directories is mechanically meaningful.  Only two field values
  differ legitimately - the runtime label, which reads Java 17 here against the
  older runtime there, and the commit descriptor.  The directory-service material
  is the one CONTENT difference, and it is carried in these notes rather than in a
  header field for exactly that reason.  A field added on one side only, or a
  region selected differently on one side only, would register as a difference
  that carries no behavioural signal at all - which is the one outcome a mirror
  exists to prevent.
